  "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"JSSP\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    # --- Data ---\n    n_jobs = 4\n    n_machines = 4\n    processing_times = [[9, 16, 23, 23], [11, 27, 27, 27], [30, 26, 28, 13], [5, 28, 22, 9]]\n    machine_assignments = [[0, 3, 1, 2], [1, 2, 0, 3], [1, 0, 3, 2], [2, 0, 3, 1]]\n\n    # Big-M: sum of all processing times\n    M = sum(processing_times[j][o] for j in range(n_jobs) for o in range(n_machines))\n\n    # --- Variables ---\n    # Start times for each operation\n    S = {}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            S[(j,o)] = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Start_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # Makespan variable\n    Cmax = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=\"Cmax\")\n\n    # Build machine -> [(job, op)] mapping\n    machine_ops = {mach: [] for mach in range(n_machines)}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            machine_ops[machine_assignments[j][o]].append((j, o))\n\n    # Disjunctive binary variables for each pair on same machine\n    Y = {}\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Y_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # --- Objective: minimize makespan ---\n    m.setObjective(Cmax, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # --- Constraints ---\n    # 1. Precedence: operations within a job must follow order\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines - 1):\n            m.addConstr(\n                S[(j,o+1)] >= S[(j,o)] + processing_times[j][o],\n                name=f\"Prec_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # 2. Disjunctive: no two operations on the same machine overlap\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                p1 = processing_times[j1][o1]\n                p2 = processing_times[j2][o2]\n                y = Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)]\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j1,o1)] + p1 <= S[(j2,o2)] + M * (1 - y),\n                    name=f\"Disj_A_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j2,o2)] + p2 <= S[(j1,o1)] + M * y,\n                    name=f\"Disj_B_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # 3. Makespan: Cmax >= completion of last operation of each job\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        last_op = n_machines - 1\n        m.addConstr(\n            Cmax >= S[(j, last_op)] + processing_times[j][last_op],\n            name=f\"Makespan_J{j}\")\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
  "LLM_description": "Subject: Coordinated production schedule for current job shop workload\n\nContext\nOur facility operates as a job shop with several specialized workstations. We are entering a planning window with multiple customer orders that must be produced. Each order (job) comprises a fixed sequence of operations that must be completed in order, across designated machines, with known processing durations. The mix of short and longer tasks, combined with shared machines, creates contention points that require a coordinated schedule rather than ad hoc dispatching.\n\nScheduling challenge\nAll operations across all jobs must be placed on the machines over time without overlap, while honoring the internal order of steps within each job. Because multiple jobs draw on the same machines and processing times vary, simple first-in-first-out rules can leave critical machines waiting or overloaded and cause jobs to sit idle between steps. The challenge is to determine the start times and sequencing of every operation across the shop so that machines are well utilized, flow is smooth, and the overall completion time is as short as possible.\n\nOperating rules and constraints\n- Fixed routing and compatibility: Every operation is tied to a specific machine. Substitutions are not permitted.\n- Known durations: Each operation has a defined processing time available in advance.\n- Machine exclusivity: Each machine can process a single operation at a time; overlapping assignments are not allowed.\n- No preemption: Once an operation begins, it runs to completion without interruption or splitting.\n- Job precedence: For each job, operations must be performed in the prescribed order; a downstream step cannot start until its predecessor finishes.\n\nSuccess measure\nOur aim is to complete all jobs in the shortest possible total time by sequencing and timing operations to minimize the finish time of the last job (makespan), while reducing idle time and unnecessary work-in-process.\n\nNext steps\n- Confirm the routing and duration details for every operation (see Annex).\n- Identify contention on shared machines and assess alternative operation sequences.\n- Build and review a feasible production schedule that respects all constraints.\n- Publish machine-by-machine start/finish times and dispatch priorities to the shop floor.\n\nAnnex \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: 4\n- Number of machines (workstations): 4\n- Job-by-job production schedule (operations must be performed in the listed sequence):\n  Job 1: Op1 on Machine 0 for 9 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 3 for 16 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 1 for 23 time units \u2192 Op4 on Machine 2 for 23 time units\n  Job 3: Op1 on Machine 1 for 30 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 0 for 26 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 3 for 28 time units \u2192 Op4 on Machine 2 for 13 time units\n  Job 2: Op1 on Machine 1 for 11 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 2 for 27 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 0 for 27 time units \u2192 Op4 on Machine 3 for 27 time units\n  Job 4: Op1 on Machine 2 for 5 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 0 for 28 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 3 for 22 time units \u2192 Op4 on Machine 1 for 9 time units",
  "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Coordinated production schedule for current job shop workload\n\nContext\nOur facility operates as a job shop with several specialized workstations. We are entering a planning window with multiple customer orders that must be produced. Each order (job) comprises a fixed sequence of operations that must be completed in order, across designated machines, with known processing durations. The mix of short and longer tasks, combined with shared machines, creates contention points that require a coordinated schedule rather than ad hoc dispatching.\n\nScheduling challenge\nAll operations across all jobs must be placed on the machines over time without overlap, while honoring the internal order of steps within each job. Because multiple jobs draw on the same machines and processing times vary, simple first-in-first-out rules can leave critical machines waiting or overloaded and cause jobs to sit idle between steps. The challenge is to determine the start times and sequencing of every operation across the shop so that machines are well utilized, flow is smooth, and the overall completion time is as short as possible.\n\nOperating rules and constraints\n- Fixed routing and compatibility: Every operation is tied to a specific machine. Substitutions are not permitted.\n- Known durations: Each operation has a defined processing time available in advance.\n- Machine exclusivity: Each machine can process a single operation at a time; overlapping assignments are not allowed.\n- No preemption: Once an operation begins, it runs to completion without interruption or splitting.\n- Job precedence: For each job, operations must be performed in the prescribed order; a downstream step cannot start until its predecessor finishes.\n\nSuccess measure\nOur aim is to complete all jobs in the shortest possible total time by sequencing and timing operations to minimize the finish time of the last job (makespan), while reducing idle time and unnecessary work-in-process.\n\nNext steps\n- Confirm the routing and duration details for every operation (see Annex).\n- Identify contention on shared machines and assess alternative operation sequences.\n- Build and review a feasible production schedule that respects all constraints.\n- Publish machine-by-machine start/finish times and dispatch priorities to the shop floor.\n\nANNEX \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: {NUM_JOBS}\n- Number of machines (workstations): {NUM_MACHINES}\n- Processing times by job and operation (time units): {PROCESSING_TIMES}\n- Machine assignments by job and operation (routing): {MACHINE_ASSIGNMENTS}"
